rned and 5,684,676 cast in person. Another 38,099,638 mail ballots sent or requested have not been returned yet.</p></details><details><summary>Which states report early votes by party?</summary><p>24 states report the party registration of early voters: Florida, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, Arizona, Colorado, New Jersey, Maryland, Nevada, New Mexico, Oregon, Maine, Iowa, Arkansas, Nebraska, Utah, Kansas, Rhode Island, South Dakota, Idaho, Wyoming, Connecticut, Kentucky, Oklahoma, Delaware. In those states registered Democrats have cast 46.3% of these ballots, Republicans 29.6% and independents and third parties 24.1%. Party registration shows who has voted, not how they voted. For states that report no party, On Point Polling estimates the party mix from county results and voter demographics.</p></details><details><summary>When is Election Day 2026?</summary><p>Election Day is Tuesday, November 3, 2026, 13 days after the latest count. Early voting dates, mail ballot deadlines and in person hours are set by each state.</p></details><details><summary>Where do these early vote numbers come from?</summary><p>Counts come from state and county election offices, compiled by civicAPI and refreshed every day. On Point Politics adds the party and demographic estimates, the comparison with 2022 and 2024 and the check against the On Point forecast.</p></details><details><summary>Does the early vote tell us who will win?</summary><p>Not by itself. Early voters lean older and more partisan than Election Day voters, and the mix changes as more ballots come in. The tracker compares each state with its own pace in 2022 and 2024 and with the On Point forecast, which is the better test of whether a state is running ahead of or behind expectations.</p></details></div></section></div></div></div><!--$--><!--/$--></div></main><footer class="opp"><div class="foot"><div class="wrap"><div class="cols"><div><div class="fbrand"><span class="lk" style="display:block"><svg viewBox="30 40 460 